<%# THE PAGE'S OWN HEADING, plus the one control that spends money.

    EVERY QUERY IS PRINTED, not merely implied by the person's name. Each is assembled
    from the person plus their team plus one variant
    (Appearances::GatherReferencePhotos#queries), so none of them is what the operator
    would have typed — and he cannot judge searches whose questions he cannot see, nor
    decide which variant to drop from a list he cannot read.

    ALL FOUR, NOT A COUNT. "4 searches" would hide the two things he actually checks
    here: that the TEAM made it into the subject (it silently did not until
    2026-09-27), and which expression words are being bought. %>
<header class="space-y-3">
  <div>
    <p class="label-upper text-muted">Photo scouting</p>
    <h1 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-heading leading-tight">
      <%= @person.full_name %>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-1">
      <%= link_to "back to #{@person.full_name}", person_path(@person.slug),
            class: "hover:text-primary underline" %>
      <% if @appearance %>
        &middot;
        <%= link_to "the look this files against", person_appearance_path(@person.slug, @appearance.slug),
              class: "hover:text-primary underline" %>
      <% end %>
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="text-sm text-body max-w-2xl">
    What the search found, what the ranking picked, and
    <strong class="text-heading">what you would have picked instead</strong>.
    Judge the search as well as the pick: every candidate it returned is below, in the
    order it returned them.
  </p>

  <% if @appearance %>
    <div class="rounded-lg border border-subtle bg-surface-alt p-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2">
      <div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
        <% if @search_available %>
          <p class="text-xs text-heading font-bold">
            Searching with <span class="font-mono"><%= @search_provider %></span>
            &middot; <%= pluralize(@search_queries.length, "query") %> per search
          </p>
          <ul data-test="search-queries" class="text-2xs text-muted break-words space-y-0.5 mt-0.5">
            <% @search_queries.each do |query| %>
              <li>
                &ldquo;<%= query %>&rdquo;
                <%# THE VARIANT, NAMED. The full query is what is bought and what is stored
                    on every row it finds, so it is what the page shows; the label beside it
                    is how the operator refers to the variant when he asks for one to change. %>
                <span class="text-3xs opacity-70">
                  <%= search_variant_label(query, subject: @search_subject) %>
                </span>
              </li>
            <% end %>
          </ul>
        <% else %>
          <p data-test="search-unconfigured" class="text-xs text-heading font-bold">
            No provider is configured
          </p>
          <p class="text-2xs text-muted break-words">
            Nothing can be searched. Set
            <span class="font-mono text-warning-ink"><%= Appearances::ImageSearch::Serper::API_KEY_ENV %></span>
            for a paid provider; the keyless one should have covered this, so its
            absence here means the registry was changed.
          </p>
        <% end %>
      </div>

      <%# ADMINS ONLY, matching the controller. A search buys ONE PROVIDER QUERY PER
          VARIANT — four of them, listed above — plus up to VISION_SHORTLIST vision
          classifications in one request, and hub signup is open, so a button shown to
          everyone is a purchase offered to everyone. A control nobody but an admin may
          use is furniture on every other reader's page. %>
      <% if admin? && @search_available %>
        <%= button_to "Search the web", search_person_scouting_path(@person.slug),
              method: :post, class: "btn btn-sm btn-primary shrink-0",
              form: { data: { test: "scouting-search-button" } } %>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</header>
